WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:05.014
In hall B a pump moves the product from a tank, through a heat exchanger,

2
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.046
to the filling unit of line 3.

3
00:00:08.031 --> 00:00:09.273
The pump makes the pressure.

4
00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:13.920
Pipes, valves and heat exchangers use it up through friction.

5
00:00:14.932 --> 00:00:17.440
Steam and cooling water are utilities.

6
00:00:18.155 --> 00:00:20.500
The process needs them but does not sell them.

7
00:00:21.505 --> 00:00:23.107
Four topics follow the pipe.

8
00:00:23.848 --> 00:00:27.191
First how the product flows, in pipes and with a pump.

9
00:00:28.295 --> 00:00:32.231
Then the heat, in heat exchangers and with steam and cooling water.

10
00:00:33.604 --> 00:00:36.472
The first topic is Flow and pressure in pipes.

11
00:00:37.186 --> 00:00:41.551
Every bend, valve and filter on the way to line 3 costs pressure.

12
00:00:42.627 --> 00:00:44.624
That loss is called the pressure drop.

13
00:00:45.349 --> 00:00:48.066
The pump has to deliver that pressure again and again,

14
00:00:48.182 --> 00:00:50.829
so every extra bend costs pump energy.

15
00:00:51.812 --> 00:00:55.667
For turbulent flow you read the friction factor from the Moody chart.

16
00:00:57.016 --> 00:00:58.734
The second topic is Pumps.

17
00:00:59.452 --> 00:01:03.400
The more pressure the pipe pushes back, the less flow the pump delivers.

18
00:01:04.412 --> 00:01:07.593
The curves of pump and pipe cross at the duty point.

19
00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:12.446
If the pump runs far from its best point there, energy is lost.

20
00:01:13.544 --> 00:01:15.286
You read the maker’s pump curve.

21
00:01:16.595 --> 00:01:20.113
The third topic is Heat transfer and heat exchangers.

22
00:01:20.849 --> 00:01:23.961
An exchanger moves heat through a wall, without mixing.

23
00:01:24.974 --> 00:01:28.271
Suppose the product has to be warm before the filling unit.

24
00:01:29.015 --> 00:01:31.430
Then it takes up heat in the exchanger.

25
00:01:32.456 --> 00:01:34.813
You often pick a plate heat exchanger.

26
00:01:36.151 --> 00:01:38.531
The fourth topic is Steam and utilities.

27
00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:43.467
Steam brings the heat to the exchanger and turns back into water there.

28
00:01:44.462 --> 00:01:48.200
That water is called condensate and has to leave the exchanger.

29
00:01:48.944 --> 00:01:50.686
Otherwise the heating drops.

30
00:01:51.733 --> 00:01:53.869
At the drain you check the steam trap.

31
00:01:54.951 --> 00:01:57.656
A trap stuck open blows live steam away.

32
00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.214
That is where energy disappears.

33
00:02:01.514 --> 00:02:02.327
The whole map.

34
00:02:03.069 --> 00:02:07.307
Pumps, flow and heat is the fifth station of Energy and the grid.

35
00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:10.120
Every topic has four levels.

36
00:02:11.116 --> 00:02:15.180
Start with the lesson on pressure, flow and pressure drop at Basics level.

37
00:02:15.928 --> 00:02:17.565
It shows where pressure goes.

38
00:02:18.625 --> 00:02:22.595
At Working knowledge level come pipe friction, pump speed control,

39
00:02:22.712 --> 00:02:24.941
heat exchangers and cooling water.

40
00:02:25.999 --> 00:02:28.437
At Advanced level come control valves,

41
00:02:28.577 --> 00:02:32.350
cavitation, exchangers that fall short and steam losses.

42
00:02:33.478 --> 00:02:36.973
At Expert level come pipe size and life-cycle cost,

43
00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:40.479
pump systems, heat recovery and the energy case.

44
00:02:41.498 --> 00:02:45.678
Every lesson and every film in this module has a place on this map.
